00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.890
So with that, let's get started.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.040
So let's take a look today.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.000
I think many of you are
familiar with the current threat

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.670
landscape today.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.010
It's actually quite
different from the way

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.010
that it looked a few years ago.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.390
In fact, the very nature
of the forces plays

00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.820
have actually shifted.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:22.480
Threats no longer originate
from rogue actors.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.270
And what we mean by
that is we now actually

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:29.200
have well-funded
organizations that actually

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.510
have organized attacks.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:36.980
So that's why we're seeing so
many of these familiar attacks,

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:42.550
whether it's phishing, malware,
social engineering, all of this

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:46.030
is increasing, not
only in volume but also

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.110
in sophistication.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.510
So what does that mean?

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.530
The growing amount spent
on information security

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:59.640
globally has been
unable to put a stop

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.670
to the rising number of
cybersecurity breaches

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.420
year over year.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.039
In fact, this year, the
security information market

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:12.590
is forecasted to
actually grow nearly 9%,

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:16.390
totaling $124 billion.

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:18.970
And the number of targeted
breaches in the US

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.230
is growing at a rate
of 27% per year.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.440
So what do we do?

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.200
This clearly is not
a winning formula

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.330
And so if we're investing more
to address the same problems

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.930
but at the same time
the number of incidents

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.160
continue to increase, then
there is a clear and painful

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:44.880
disconnect.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:50.210
This is not going to end well.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.310
Simply throwing
money at this problem

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.280
is not going to solve it.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:00.350
So let's take a look at our
current endpoint strategy.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:04.910
To combat these actual risks,
organizations are ultimately

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:08.900
seeking endpoint
devices that use

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.340
an OS with an embedded
security module, one

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:18.950
that is inherently resistant
to viruses, as well as malware,

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.630
by design.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:23.120
This enables automatic
support for safety measures,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:26.720
such as two factor
authentication, smart card

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.860
readers, and many more
security features.

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:34.520
So I want you all to think
about your endpoint computing

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.820
strategy.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:36.620
And here's why.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:41.340
So let's think for
a moment in time,

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.680
instead of managing an entire
fleet of inherently at risk

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.770
endpoint devices, I
want you all to think

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:53.690
about how you can actually
control the threat by moving

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:56.900
that endpoint to the cloud.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.160
And you can do this by using
an actual secure cloud native

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.310
solution like Chrome Enterprise.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:09.500
Chrome devices as well as Chrome
browser are secure by design.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:13.580
They deter end users
from falling hostage

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:15.320
to harmful attacks.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:17.880
And in the event that
one actually prevails,

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.655
it actually
mitigates the impact.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.680
So let's talk about what we mean
when I say secure by design.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.300
Chrome devices are
designed from the inception

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.610
to be fundamentally different.

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:38.300
It's a different platform,
different operating system.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.810
They're not created by
simply throwing together

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:46.820
an OS on a generic
commoditized motherboard

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.620
or a piece of hardware.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.570
And so what we mean
by that is regardless

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:53.930
of which OEM manufacturer
that you select,

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.790
whether it be HP,
Samsung, Asus, et cetera,

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.330
they all build to designs that
are architected by Google.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:05.690
So we dictate the
reference design

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:09.500
that we use to different
OEMs of choice.

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.800
And with the specific
hardware components

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.360
that are engineered
to work, they

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.420
work together in sync
with the operating system

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:18.900
and vise versa.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.860
So each layer of the
machine you're working with

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:24.770
works in conjunction
with others in a way that

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:31.000
provides a unique and ultimately
a very secure environment.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.660
So when you start using
Chrome devices at work,

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:38.940
your work is ultimately
stored safely in the cloud.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.740
Now given the nature
of the situation,

00:05:41.740 --> 00:05:43.830
some files, whether
it's your user

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:49.120
data, your downloaded files,
your cookies, your cache, et

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.800
cetera, they need to be
present on the actual device,

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.270
for example.

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.260
Chromebooks natively
encrypt this data,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.680
leveraging a built-in
Google security module.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:07.320
In fact, encryption is enabled
by default on a Chromebook

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.300
and can not be disabled.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:14.370
This is regardless of anybody
that uses that device.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.980
The hardware has a
tamper-resistant security

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.780
module built in.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.787
And this is on
every Chrome device

00:06:20.787 --> 00:06:21.870
that's manufactured today.

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.750
So the security hardware
does more than just

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.820
ultimately encrypt
the actual data.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.960
It performs a multitude of
tasks to prevent software

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:35.740
and firmware version rollbacks.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.880
It provides tamper evidence.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.880
And a good example
of that is someone

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:42.820
that actually tries to
swap the hardware out,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:47.020
like a hard drive, with a
different piece of hardware

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.090
Additionally, there
are hardware components

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.030
that serve to protect
the OS tampering.

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:54.700
All Chromebooks use something
called verified boot,

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.160
if you're not
familiar with that.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:59.290
A verified boot confirms
that the operating system

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:04.400
is authentic, safe, and
is Google-distributed

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:05.990
in leveraging a build.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.870
You can not boot a
corrupted version

00:07:08.870 --> 00:07:11.010
of the operating system.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.830
In fact, there's
actually two versions

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:18.350
of the operating system on
the device at all times.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.360
Through the verified
boot process,

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:23.030
if the OS is found
to be corrupted,

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:27.210
it can switch to the
ultimately the second version

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:31.200
of the operating system, which
provides the verified boot

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.220
check.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:35.730
And additionally, having another
version of the OS automatically

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:40.230
means that you can update
the background procedure

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:44.070
for the operating system without
interrupting the actual user's

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:44.820
work.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.680
So by having two versions
of the operating system,

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.410
you can do updates
in the background,

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:53.010
and you can ultimately
update the actual device

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:57.000
during the session
which a user is using.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.200
There are other
benefits to having

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:03.390
an actual small footprint
of data on the device.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:06.390
First off, Chromebooks are
cloud native by design.

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:10.470
So unlike traditional laptops,
your files and your data

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:13.590
are primarily
stored in the cloud.

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:18.700
Chromebooks can be set up
in an ephemeral fashion.

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:22.080
So for those of you that don't
know what ephemeral means,

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:27.090
this means that all user data is
wiped clean after each session,

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:31.380
whether you can set
a timer, you can

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.039
wipe the device after a
user selects the sign out

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.530
or exit option.

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:41.640
With Chrome devices, your
data is kept inaccessible,

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.340
it's kept offline, and it's
ultimately kept secure.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:50.000
Furthermore, let's take a
look with Chrome devices,

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.540
there is no longer this
need to continually attend

00:08:53.540 --> 00:08:57.020
to update releases and patches.

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:01.310
For anything, as I previously
mentioned, updates to Chrome

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:06.200
occur in the background
without direct interaction

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.450
on behalf of the user.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:10.920
So we're ultimately
as an administrative,

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:14.480
we're not pestering or
nagging the user with all

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:16.370
of these pop ups and reminders.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:20.390
Administrators can
rest assured easily

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.660
manage their fleet of
Chrome enterprise devices

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.820
and ensure that they
have the most up to date

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.730
and secure version of
the operating system

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:30.770
in their fleet of machines.

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.210
It's important to
ultimately note

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:36.710
that Chrome is in a constant
state of improvement.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:40.190
And with continual bug
fixes, security enhancements,

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:44.420
and feature rollouts
on a frequent basis,

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:49.520
we keep abreast of the current
threat landscape that occurs

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:52.550
on Chrome Enterprise today.

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.400
Chrome comes with the option
of leveraging Google's Safe

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.410
Browsing service.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:01.190
This is the same service that's
available on the consumer

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.610
side of things.

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.000
But on the Enterprise
side, what we do

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:08.380
is Google indexes over
trillions of websites

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:11.360
and maintains a
database of sites

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.960
that are known or
suspected to be malicious.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.960
In the event that there
is a malicious site,

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:22.460
we'll alert the user
about the actual site.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.890
And we'll actually give the
option to proceed or not.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.500
Administrators actually
from Enterprise

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.170
enrolled devices
have the ability

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:35.070
to prevent users from going
to those sites altogether.

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:41.420
So this notion of leveraging
Google's scope and scale has

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:45.690
helped kept malicious
apps off devices,

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:48.470
which is present in many
of the aspect features

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.170
that we offer today from a
Chrome Enterprise perspective.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:56.000
So Chrome offers a multitude
of Enterprise applications.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.370
So if you take a look at
all the different ways

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:02.480
to extend beyond the browser,
some of the native features

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:06.240
with Chrome browser include
the ability to, let's say,

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:10.530
run a customized Google
Play Store front.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:13.320
A lot of enterprises
want these features.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:14.790
In addition to
that, you can also

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:20.550
access legacy applications, such
as VDI Solutions from partners

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:23.690
like Citrix, as well as VMware.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:27.410
While still others can run
using Chrome's native ability,

00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:30.680
we also have the ability
to run Android and Linux

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:36.410
applications on many other
types of devices as well.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:41.410
So Chrome browser has long used
sandboxing to actually contain

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.950
threats with browser
tabs Chrome apps

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.370
and the overall Chrome
browser process.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:52.090
Chromebooks takes this
to an actual further step

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:57.220
by ensuring that all Android,
Linux, and OS processes

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.640
are kept separate and
contained from each other.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.160
So Google recently
actually introduced

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.860
site isolation, which provides
additional protection, making

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:12.760
it even harder for
untrustworthy websites

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:16.960
to access or steal information
from your accounts,

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:20.260
as well as other sites.

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:25.228
So now with all those extensions
that you run on Chrome,

00:12:25.228 --> 00:12:27.020
how do we actually know
that we're actually

00:12:27.020 --> 00:12:31.250
vetting and securing
these actual extensions?

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.140
So what Google does
behind the scenes

00:12:33.140 --> 00:12:38.810
is we constantly enforce
a rigorous control

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:42.590
on what's available in the
Chrome Web Store today.

00:12:42.590 --> 00:12:48.410
We implement processes such as
extension reviews, both manual

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.600
as well as automated.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:54.320
Some of these might include
fingerprint analysis,

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.150
reputation analysis.

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:01.280
And we also have added a new
feature which no longer accepts

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.410
obfuscated JavaScript files.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:08.630
So let's take a look at
the management policies.

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.420
Chrome provides
admins the ability

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:15.260
to enforce very, very
granular controls.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:19.850
These controls offer
administrators exactly what

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:23.990
they can and cannot enforce
to be installed and run

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.300
on a Chromebook.

00:13:26.300 --> 00:13:31.400
As an admin, I can dictate
which policies and app or apps

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.560
can be permitted
to be installed,

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.970
which are automatically
installed,

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:39.850
which apps can be also
force installed and pinned

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:41.750
to the toolbar.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:47.140
I can also blacklist apps which
can be forbidden altogether.

00:13:47.140 --> 00:13:48.640
This extends
further to determine

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:52.330
which sites can perform what
actions or even prevents users

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:55.910
from visiting specific sites.

00:13:55.910 --> 00:13:59.090
Lastly, if you enter your
corporate password on a site

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:01.640
that isn't corporate-owned--

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.220
we actually use this
at Google today--

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:07.280
but you'll actually
get a notification

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:11.240
that your password was
used on a third party site.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.990
This is a new feature, which
eliminates potential phishing

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:17.860
usage by tricking a
user into entering

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:19.610
their corporate
credentials on a site that

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:24.430
might appear to be legitimate.

00:14:24.430 --> 00:14:26.580
Now let's talk about
Google Play Protect.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:30.330
So Play Protect scans all
the apps in the app store

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:34.740
by leveraging our AI fed
billions of data points.

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.480
We look at everything
from pinpointing

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:41.790
where to look to which results
in a very low number of PHA,

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.090
or Potentially Harmful Apps.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:49.710
So Play Protect also utilizes
something called attestation.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.860
This is a SafetyNET
Attestation API.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:56.340
It provides app
developers the ability

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:59.100
to determine if their
app is actually running

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:02.460
on a genuine Android platform.

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:05.130
And ultimately,
developers can also

00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:08.990
use the Verify
Apps APIs to query

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.410
the status of Play
Protect for mitigation

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.260
as well as remediation.

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:20.390
So let's take a look at how all
of these pieces come together

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:24.800
and work together, which
ensures that we protect

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:28.820
the users and their data,
as well as your data

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:29.990
and how we keep you safe.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:35.610
So here's a common
attack for phishing.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:39.200
In this scenario,
a phishing attacker

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:41.750
will conduct research by
gathering information,

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:44.960
let's say, about your
corporate employees.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:49.220
They might even craft a
highly targeted email,

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:52.050
tricking employees and
users into thinking, hey,

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.470
this is a legitimate message.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:56.870
Employees are scammed
into downloading

00:15:56.870 --> 00:15:59.150
malicious attachments.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:00.830
They might even give
up their password

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:04.640
or share sensitive data.

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:11.110
So how do we address this by
stopping the actual scams?

00:16:11.110 --> 00:16:14.140
We do actually three
things on the platform.

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.020
First off, if you're
leveraging Gmail,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:20.230
we use machine learning to
identify phishing emails

00:16:20.230 --> 00:16:24.010
and prevent them from
reaching inboxes altogether.

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:26.710
But let's say you're
not using Gmail.

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:29.800
Chrome warns employees
before clicking

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:35.140
on suspicious links that leads
to dangerous actual sites

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:40.210
Additionally, security
keys help prevent hackers

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:44.907
from using stolen passwords
to access employee accounts.

00:16:44.907 --> 00:16:45.740
Real quick question.

00:16:45.740 --> 00:16:47.615
I probably won't be able
to see you all here.

00:16:47.615 --> 00:16:49.930
But quick show of hands,
how many folks out there

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:53.170
are actually using hardware
security keys today?

00:16:56.760 --> 00:16:59.530
Very few.

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:04.130
So let's take a look at an
actual second common attack,

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:05.190
ransomware.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.829
I think many of you
are familiar with this.

00:17:07.829 --> 00:17:12.660
With ransomware, things
are a bit different.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.589
Ransomware is designed to block
access to a computer system

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:19.380
until a sum of money is paid.

00:17:19.380 --> 00:17:21.660
So out of the gate,
I want you all

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:26.190
to remember that the Chromebook
data model limits the data that

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:29.770
can be held at ransom
since very, very little,

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:34.428
if anything is designed to be
on the device to begin with.

00:17:34.428 --> 00:17:35.970
And what we mean by
that additionally

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:41.310
is that executables, MSI
files, they can't be installed,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.400
they can't run.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:47.420
They can't run locally plain
and simple on a Chromebook.

00:17:47.420 --> 00:17:50.690
We talked a good deal about
malicious applications earlier.

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:52.970
And just a bit ago, we
even discussed the security

00:17:52.970 --> 00:17:57.020
and protection controls that
we use in Google Play as well

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.030
as the Chrome Web Store.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:00.800
And I want to
highlight that even

00:18:00.800 --> 00:18:06.890
in the unlikely event of an
exploit, the scope is contained

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.370
and its impact limited by
Chrome's inherent sandboxing

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:11.870
model.

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:16.030
Here's a really
interesting question

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:17.560
that I get all the time.

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:25.710
The question that comes up is,
why do I not need antivirus

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:26.710
on my Chromebook device?

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:33.350
So a quick explanation of
this is much conventional

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:35.630
wisdom has led us
to the conclusion

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:38.490
that in order to
secure our devices,

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:42.290
we need to basically
have a stack of product

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:45.710
to assist with securing
that actual device.

00:18:45.710 --> 00:18:50.210
If your genuine interest is in
keeping your operating systems

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:54.980
virus free, I think that they
ask and what we've done here

00:18:54.980 --> 00:19:00.440
is design one in
which viruses can not

00:19:00.440 --> 00:19:03.660
install on themselves
to begin with,

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:06.980
which is exactly
what Chrome OS is.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:11.120
Not only does this result in
inherently more secure devices

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:16.220
by design, but it saves you a
boatload of virus protection

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:17.720
subscription licenses overall.

00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:23.830
And of course, because the
threat landscape is very, very

00:19:23.830 --> 00:19:31.670
dynamic, continually evolving
and changing, it's critical,

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:34.690
I think, for all of us to
keep our devices in your fleet

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:37.000
as current as possible.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.240
So as we discussed
earlier, the background

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.850
updating capability
of Chrome OS ensures

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:46.390
that this happens
seamlessly, it happens

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:49.210
without any direct
interaction, and it

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:52.600
happens on behalf
of the end user,

00:19:52.600 --> 00:19:55.030
because administrators
have the ability

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:57.430
to dictate when this
actually happens.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:00.430
This gives everybody
in the enterprise

00:20:00.430 --> 00:20:08.870
the maximum amount of protection
with minimal, if any effort.

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:12.350
And with that, I'd like
to welcome Daniel McClure

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:15.740
from Warby Parker on the stage.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:17.490
DANIEL MCCLURE: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:20:17.490 --> 00:20:20.210
As Chester mentioned,
I'm Daniel McClure,

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:23.910
manager of tech services I've
been with the company for five

00:20:23.910 --> 00:20:24.410
years now.

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:27.680
I just celebrated my five
year anniversary on Sunday.

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:29.480
So technology
services is the team

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.220
that's responsible
for managing the users

00:20:31.220 --> 00:20:35.870
and devices across two corporate
locations, one in New York City

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:38.720
and one in Nashville, a
manufacturing facility

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:40.460
that's about an hour
and a half north

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:44.270
of New York City where
we actually cut and pop

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:46.880
our lenses into frames.

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:51.960
And then we are close to 100
retail locations as of today.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:55.310
So right now, the landscape
looks like about 2,000 users,

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:58.250
3,500 different
endpoint devices,

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.100
225 internet connections
that are all trying

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:04.340
to be down as much as possible.

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:07.010
And I have a team of
five support engineers.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:08.510
So one of the reasons
that I've been

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:12.830
able to keep such a
lean, efficient team

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:16.580
is that over the
past five years.

00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:19.340
I've tried to ingrain a
culture of self-service,

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:24.860
really empowering the
end user using anything

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:29.030
I can grab from my
toolkit to really leverage

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:33.170
and remove any kind of
blockers that the typical IT

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.990
manager would see,
anything like I

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:38.270
don't have admin
access on my computer,

00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:41.760
I need access to more services,
I need my accounts unlocked

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:43.490
and my passwords reset.

00:21:43.490 --> 00:21:47.090
So again, really pushing
a lot of the work

00:21:47.090 --> 00:21:50.960
back onto the user to kind
of remove IT as a blocker

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:54.060
from getting things done.

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:58.460
So I never wanted to
be an IT team that

00:21:58.460 --> 00:21:59.660
is an impedance to work.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:03.690
I want to help push
people into the future.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:06.440
The problem with this
is that as we push users

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:10.460
into a more self-service
and user empowering model,

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:13.610
we have to do so very
thoughtfully to make sure

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:16.970
that we're striking just that
perfect balance between user

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:19.580
experience and security.

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:22.760
And I think that we've done
a really good job internally.

00:22:22.760 --> 00:22:25.190
So a few of the
things that we're

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:28.760
doing that have simultaneously
increased our users' experience

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:31.520
but also increased
our security posture

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.820
would be something like,
how we onboard a user.

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:37.880
So unlike other
management platforms,

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:42.020
if you're using G Suite, G
Suite Enterprise and Chrome

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:45.020
Enterprise, not only do
you have the availability

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:48.380
to manage the device itself
by ensuring that it's

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:51.320
fully encrypted, the screen is
locking after a certain period

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:53.840
of time, and that it's booting
into an operating system

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:56.750
that it should be booting into
that hasn't been tampered with,

00:22:56.750 --> 00:23:01.730
you're also able to manage
the experience from a user

00:23:01.730 --> 00:23:03.060
perspective as well.

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:07.270
So as an example, on day
one, a Warby Parker employee

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:09.160
will start.

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:11.960
They'll come into the
office, they'll get the tour,

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:14.090
and their G Suite
user is sitting

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:18.800
in an organization or a unit
that has no access to anything.

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.620
They can look Gmail, they
can look at Calendar,

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:23.660
and they can change
some of their settings.

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:26.150
And we do this because if the
credentials for these newly

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:29.430
created accounts ever get
out, it's a big, big problem.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:32.080
So we just want to silo them
off as much as possible.

00:23:32.080 --> 00:23:33.920
The first introduction,
the first course

00:23:33.920 --> 00:23:37.130
that somebody goes through in
their tenure at Warby Parker

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:40.940
is a class where they're
led through an introduction

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.280
to the hardware, and
introduction to Google,

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.920
and then enrolling
their user into

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:48.750
multi-factor authentication.

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:52.760
So within the first
few hours that they've

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.700
been with Warby Parker,
they understand,

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:57.650
they've been explained
to what multi-factor

00:23:57.650 --> 00:24:02.060
is, they are more incentivized
to go home and enable

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:04.260
two factor authentication
on their own services.

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.710
But as a company,
because we enforce

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:08.480
this across the
entire domain, we're

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:15.680
just much more secure at
that day one crossroad.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:19.520
So all of this happening
within the first couple hours,

00:24:19.520 --> 00:24:22.280
we then moved their
user into an OU that

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:25.940
has more departmental
specific access,

00:24:25.940 --> 00:24:29.420
and they have everything they
need to get off and going.

00:24:29.420 --> 00:24:32.120
Something else that we've done,
because of the security posture

00:24:32.120 --> 00:24:33.680
of these devices
and the policies

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:37.280
that we are pushing down
into Chrome, we do--

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:38.730
let me back up a little bit.

00:24:38.730 --> 00:24:40.700
I mentioned, we have
a Nashville office.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:43.720
It's about 350 people that
work in the Nashville area.

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:47.707
And 120 of those work
solely from home.

00:24:47.707 --> 00:24:49.040
They never come into the office.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:53.150
So there's a big piece of this
picture that we cannot manage,

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:55.130
and that is their
internet connection.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.380
So we can push as many
policies to the device

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.550
as we want in the
user's account.

00:24:59.550 --> 00:25:02.600
But if somebody has a
slow internet connection,

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:05.130
there's really nothing
we can do about that.

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:09.560
So typically, VPN has been
a big friction point in IT,

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:12.140
anything from needing
to request access

00:25:12.140 --> 00:25:13.910
and submitting a
ticket, getting software

00:25:13.910 --> 00:25:16.790
installed, and then
also that penalty

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:19.940
that it can take on your
internet connection.

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.940
Typically, everybody has a
fast internet connection.

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:26.870
So we really don't see
that speed penalty.

00:25:26.870 --> 00:25:29.730
But for a lot of our
users working from home,

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:34.060
they might be limited to like a
megabyte down or a megabit down

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:37.610
and 0.5 up, so very slow
internet connection.

00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:40.860
Adding that penalty onto
it is just untenable.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.430
So again to loop back
around, because we're

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:46.490
able to manage these
devices at such granularity,

00:25:46.490 --> 00:25:50.480
we can ensure their full disk
encryption, verified booth.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:53.450
We have all agreed
internally that it's

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:56.150
OK to start moving some
of these private services

00:25:56.150 --> 00:26:01.770
out into the public, making
sure that HTTPS is everywhere,

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:03.870
multi-factor is on.

00:26:03.870 --> 00:26:06.080
And we can allow these
work from home users

00:26:06.080 --> 00:26:09.140
to do what they have to do
without the need of a VPN.

00:26:11.568 --> 00:26:13.610
Shifting gears a little
bit to talk about retail,

00:26:13.610 --> 00:26:15.680
in our retail
environment, we probably

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:19.820
ship three to four laptops
to each individual store.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:23.150
And these stores can range from
the number of employees from 25

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:24.360
on up to 50.

00:26:24.360 --> 00:26:27.740
So it's very important as
shifts change throughout the day

00:26:27.740 --> 00:26:32.650
to have these shared devices
within our retail stores.

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:36.170
We, using other
operating systems,

00:26:36.170 --> 00:26:39.390
have tried to have this very
shared environment where

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:41.210
somebody comes in the
morning, they take

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:43.010
a laptop off the charging rack.

00:26:43.010 --> 00:26:46.230
Somebody comes in midday,
they need to grab a laptop.

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:49.820
they have to do front of
house back to house duties.

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:53.000
There was a laptop assignment
where like two to three

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:56.330
people were assigned to
each individual laptop.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.530
This caused a lot of issues.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:03.830
So we were able to replace
those with Chrome OS and Chrome

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:07.400
devices because of that out
of the box shared experience

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.430
while maintaining this
full disk encryption,

00:27:10.430 --> 00:27:12.260
screen lock after
about a minute.

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:16.250
Keep in mind that these
associates are interacting

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:19.010
with systems that touch PHI.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:22.640
So everything that we do has to
be very mindful around keeping

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:24.920
within HIPAA compliance.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:26.940
So that's really
allowed us to, again,

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:30.560
keep a very low number of
laptops out in the wild

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:32.390
and have that shared
experience as well,

00:27:32.390 --> 00:27:34.730
because anybody with
a Warby Parker account

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:37.160
can authenticate.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:39.440
So while we're
doing this, we also

00:27:39.440 --> 00:27:44.270
have to be very aware of these
three big threat vectors that

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.160
are going on right now.

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:49.410
And as we get around
solving for one,

00:27:49.410 --> 00:27:52.010
the others is just move
into the back of our head

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:54.320
until it comes in
shoulder taps us and says,

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:55.490
they want to exploit.

00:27:55.490 --> 00:27:57.960
You need to do all these things.

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:04.730
So Warby Parker, kind of being
born in the era of like SAS

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:08.930
based services, malware has
always kind of been top of mind

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:10.760
.

00:28:10.760 --> 00:28:15.800
And with the ability to
block any kind of potentially

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.710
nefarious extensions, something
that could potentially

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:22.310
be exfiltrating data,
anything that kind of bubbles

00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:28.160
up, any kind of security alert
that our legal team may come

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:30.770
across their plate and
they request that we block

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:35.180
these extensions has been very,
very helpful, very beneficial.

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:37.220
And also, when it
comes to malware,

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:39.580
because everything is
siloed and sandboxed

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:41.180
into its own little realm--

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:44.780
each Chrome browser tab
is its own environment--

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:49.430
if any incident is occurring
on one individual tab,

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:51.750
it will not proliferate
out into the rest

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:55.130
and into the operating system.

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:57.350
As an example of something
that kind of shoulder taps

00:28:57.350 --> 00:28:59.540
you as a day one,
there was the Chrome

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:03.320
exploit about a month ago
where everybody was saying this

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:05.600
is a zero day, you must update.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:08.600
We were able to mitigate
this risk very quickly

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:12.770
by going into G Suite,
unpinning the operating system,

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:14.610
and forcing all the
devices to update.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.180
So something like that really
saves on a lot of engineering

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:18.430
resources.

00:29:18.430 --> 00:29:20.780
Credential threat kind of
goes hand in hand a little bit

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:21.920
with malware.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.620
Again, we just want to make
sure that all of our devices

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:26.305
are running the latest
version of Chrome

00:29:26.305 --> 00:29:27.680
and that they're
actually booting

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:32.450
into a version of Chrome OS
that has not been modified,

00:29:32.450 --> 00:29:34.940
hasn't been tampered
with just to ensure

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:38.462
that they're coming into
our systems very securely.

00:29:38.462 --> 00:29:39.920
And then of course,
phishing, which

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:42.050
is kind of the big hot topic.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:45.110
I think I read yesterday
in another session

00:29:45.110 --> 00:29:49.740
that 90% of all data leaks
start with a phishing attack.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:52.940
So again ensuring that
devices are just up to date,

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:58.610
regularly checking in any
devices that we may use for any

00:29:58.610 --> 00:30:01.610
kind of digital signage, making
sure that they're locked down

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:06.800
and cannot be interacted with as
someone may just naturally move

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.020
to one and start
checking their email.

00:30:09.020 --> 00:30:11.350
We want to make sure that
each device has its own place

00:30:11.350 --> 00:30:14.560
and is doing the things
that should be doing.

00:30:14.560 --> 00:30:16.810
And we now have the
option of choosing

00:30:16.810 --> 00:30:21.548
what flavor of multi-factor
we'd like to enforce across,

00:30:21.548 --> 00:30:22.840
which ones we'd like to revoke.

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:27.040
So very recently, we can now
turn off as a SMS multi-factor

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:28.250
authentication.

00:30:28.250 --> 00:30:30.920
So NEST has come out
and essentially said,

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:33.880
SMS is no longer secure.

00:30:33.880 --> 00:30:35.270
It's better than nothing.

00:30:35.270 --> 00:30:39.640
But it's still as you move
into the different levels

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:43.480
of different multi-factor,
the user experience

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:47.000
actually gets much better
as they get more secure.

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:51.340
So SMS being less secure, the
user experience isn't great.

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:53.200
You have the
Authenticator application,

00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:55.030
which the user
experience improves

00:30:55.030 --> 00:30:57.340
and security improves
at the same time.

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:59.590
You have the screen prompt
where you unlock your phone

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:00.820
and tap Yes.

00:31:00.820 --> 00:31:03.703
Again, user experience
improves, security improves,

00:31:03.703 --> 00:31:05.620
and hopefully you know
the dream at some point

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:08.290
is actually get U2F
tokens everywhere

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:10.060
where it's just a
touch of a button

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:12.110
and you're into that system.

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:14.950
So again, there's
this common thought

00:31:14.950 --> 00:31:17.350
that as you're
increasing security,

00:31:17.350 --> 00:31:19.150
the user experience
drops, and you

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:25.120
have to negotiate with yourself
and make exceptions somewhere.

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:28.450
But we've organically
moved into this realm where

00:31:28.450 --> 00:31:31.190
we're increasing the user's
experience by granting them

00:31:31.190 --> 00:31:34.030
the access they need, but
improving security by doing it

00:31:34.030 --> 00:31:35.860
in a very smart way.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:41.410
So all of this to say,
Chrome OS at Warby Parker

00:31:41.410 --> 00:31:45.340
is the most popular
computer operating system.

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:47.830
We have well over 1,000 devices.

00:31:47.830 --> 00:31:54.010
And it has helped us scale
in a very secure way.

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:56.590
So there's one big
achievement there.

00:31:56.590 --> 00:31:59.560
The second big
achievement is sitting

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:03.220
on the BeyondCorp model,
the VPN-less model.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:08.950
And it has helped us to decrease
our VPN use by close to 90%.

00:32:08.950 --> 00:32:11.110
We certainly have teams
that still rely on it

00:32:11.110 --> 00:32:16.300
and still need it, but we're
working on that remaining 10%.

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:20.617
So that is what I have
to say about that.

00:32:20.617 --> 00:32:22.450
CHESTER LUI: So in the
event that you really

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:26.050
like this movie, please
visit our website

00:32:26.050 --> 00:32:28.600
if you have any questions
on Enterprise security

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:30.610
as it relates to
Chrome Enterprise.

00:32:30.610 --> 00:32:33.660
